<!-- cell: title-page id="980a31f2-74ad-4680-aeaa-9d0218c30862" title="Notes in the Pockets" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="8c537b62-67c5-4546-bc77-f615c9629475" -->

The Hollis Street laundromat never closed. Its red neon sign was the only lit window between the hospital and Mara's flat, and inside it was always warm and humming.

Mara came on Tuesdays at three in the morning, after twelve hours on the cardiac ward of holding hands and being kind and being quick and being right. In the laundromat nobody needed anything from her, and she could sit and watch the machines turn.

The owner, Felix, sat behind the counter doing crosswords in pen and wearing a name badge he seemed to have forgotten. By the window stood an empty green armchair that he wiped down every night and that no customer was ever allowed to sit in.

One Tuesday her basket came back from the dryer with a man's flannel shirt folded on top, and in its pocket was a note: Sorry. Wrong basket. Yours is the one with the cats on the pyjamas. Excellent cats. — F.

She laughed out loud, alone, at half past three, the first time she had laughed in weeks. The next week she left a reply in the pocket of her scrubs: The cats send their regards. They want to know what eleven across was.

It went on like that all winter: notes in pockets, notes in sleeves, once a note in the toe of a sock. He told her about the crossword, and she told him about the patient who had proposed to her and then forgotten her name. They never said more than good evening to each other's faces.

In March the laundromat went dark. A sign on the door said CLOSED FOR RENOVATION. Mara stood under the dead neon with her basket on her hip and understood how much of her week had been built around a slip of paper.

For three Tuesdays she washed her clothes in the hospital basement, where the machines were silent, the air smelled of bleach, and she was once again on call.

On the fourth Tuesday the neon was back, with a note taped beneath it: Reopening tonight. New dryers. Old crosswords. Please come in.

Inside, everything gleamed. The green armchair had been moved from the window to the counter, and beside it stood a second chair.

"It was my wife's," Felix said, before she could ask. "She used to sit there and tell me which answers I'd got wrong. I couldn't move it for six years." He looked at the chairs, then at her. "Eleven across," he said. "Eight letters. Being alone."

"Solitude," said Mara, and sat down beside him.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="8170c1af-228e-485f-8cb1-c33eae605d35" title="Mara" words="29-73, 159-201, 224-246, 273-299, 300-327, 419-426" -->

A night-shift cardiac nurse who gives all her care away at work and has none left for herself. She wants somewhere no one needs anything from her, and fears losing what little she has.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="b4526ea5-625b-454e-903e-85172f954ad8" title="Felix" words="74-118, 143-158, 224-246, 354-372, 373-418" -->

The widowed owner of an all-night laundromat: gentle, wry and stuck in grief. He keeps his late wife's armchair empty and hides behind crosswords, until notes in pockets let him say what he cannot say aloud.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="62f55988-034d-48be-8330-93b3149a3b9e" title="The Hollis Street laundromat" words="0-28, 57-73, 258-272, 328-350, 351-353" -->

An all-night laundromat whose warm, humming room is the only lit window on Mara's way home: a refuge where nobody expects anything of anyone.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="65b74b8e-3c35-400e-b0d3-8018e16efa4c" title="Loneliness" words="29-56, 95-118, 159-175, 247-257, 273-299, 300-327, 373-418" -->

Two people each alone and never saying so: the empty armchair, the visits at three in the morning, a week built around a slip of paper, and the crossword answer 'solitude'.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="1aa143fa-0f7b-4329-b76d-73155ab8a9d3" title="Love" words="119-158, 159-201, 202-257, 328-350, 354-372, 403-418, 419-426" -->

Love growing between Mara and Felix through notes left in pockets, without a word said in person, until he makes room for her beside his wife's chair.

<!-- cell: plot id="4141420d-5a50-4ea2-8d55-e00e20d71268" title="Notes in the pockets" nodes="n1:119-158, n2:159-201, n3:202-223, n4:258-299, n5:328-350, n6:354-372, n7:403-418, n8:419-426" -->

A mixed-up basket starts an exchange of notes; when the laundromat closes Mara is bereft, until a note on the door brings her back for the reopening and she sits down beside Felix.

<!-- cell: plot id="5c0e06b8-0a95-4303-94ef-b3856aca947d" title="Felix's grief" nodes="n1:74-118, n2:354-372, n3:373-418, n4:419-426" -->

Felix has kept his late wife's chair empty for six years; at the reopening he moves it to the counter beside a second chair, tells Mara why, and she sits down.
